CLAIMS=[
('Young people have 86% less purchasing power than boomers had in their 20s; they need almost $7 for what a boomer bought for $1.','86% less purchasing power',C+'1 / (1 - 0.86) = $7.14, so the internal arithmetic is consistent. '+R+'The 86% figure is a widely recirculated internet statistic with no identifiable primary study; real median incomes of young adults are higher than in the 1970s-80s, so the claim is unsupported and likely false as stated. '+U,[]),
('1988: median home $110,000, median household income ~$22,700, ratio 4:1.','the median home price was $110,000',P+'FRED: MSPUS 1988 Q1 $110,000 (Q1-Q2; $115,000 in Q3) verified. MEHOINUSA646N 1988 nominal median household income $27,230, not $22,700. '+C+'110,000 / 27,230 = 4.04, so the 4:1 ratio is right even though the stated income is wrong (110,000 / 22,700 would be 4.85).',['FREDMSP','FREDINC']),
('2022: median home $433,000, median income ~$70,000, ratio over 6:1.','The median home price had skyrocketed to $433,000',P+'FRED: MSPUS 2022 quarterly mean $432,950 (verified ~$433,000). MEHOINUSA646N 2022 $74,580, not ~$70,000. '+C+'432,950 / 74,580 = 5.81, so "over 6:1" is overstated (6.19 only with the understated income).',['FREDMSP','FREDINC']),
('A ratio of 5 is healthy; the 2006-2008 bubble hit 7; we are now above bubble levels; the UK has been over 7 for a decade, lately around 9.','home price to income ratio of five',C+'Using the same FRED series, 2022 is 5.8, below the "7" the script assigns to the bubble, so "above bubble levels" contradicts its own framing; ratios depend on series choice. '+R+'UK ONS ratios ~8-9 in England recently are consistent; not fetched.',['FREDMSP','FREDINC']),
('BLS: $100,000 on a home in 1990 equals ~$377,000 today; general goods ~$231,000.','would only require about $231,000 today',P+'FRED CPIAUCSL: 1990 average 130.658; September 2025 324.245, a 2.48x multiplier, so $100,000 in 1990 = ~$248,000 at upload ($231,000 matches about 2021-2022). '+R+'The $377,000 home figure has no stated series; MSPUS rose ~3.4x from 1990 to 2025. Direction (homes outpacing CPI) correct.',['FREDCPI']),
('Pew: about 45% of millennials do not live with a family of their own vs 31% for earlier generations and 15% for the Silent generation.','more than four out of 10 millennials',R+'Pew Research (2018-2020 generational comparisons) reported figures of this kind by recall; exact numbers not fetched. '+U,[]),
('Tuition $30-50K a year; student debt $1.7T; tuition more than doubled and average graduate balance tripled ($10K to $30K) 1991-2021; ~7% owe over $100K; graduates up 250% since 1970.','Student debt has ballooned into a $1.7 trillion crisis',R+'Fed/ED federal and private student debt ~$1.6-1.8T and ~7% of borrowers over $100K match by recall. The tuition range applies to private and out-of-state (in-state public ~$11-12K). Other figures not checked. '+U,[]),
('Average car payment over $700 with ~6-year terms; young drivers pay $200-300/month for insurance (~$4,000/year); gas costs 40-60% more relative to wages.','The average car payment today is over $700',R+'Edmunds new-car averages over $700 and ~68-70-month terms are consistent by recall. '+C+'$300 x 12 = $3,600, so "nearly $4,000" is an upper bound. The wage-adjusted gas claim is not supported. '+U,[]),
('Card APRs are 20-25%; experts recommend saving 15-20% for retirement; raising a child to 18 costs over $250,000; healthcare costs tripled in a decade.','estimated to cost over $250,000',R+'Fed G.19 card rates ~21-22% and the USDA 2015 estimate ($233,610, ~$310K inflation-adjusted) are consistent. "Healthcare tripled in a decade" contradicts CMS data (national health spending grew ~60% 2013-2023); likely false. '+U,[]),
('Many first-time buyers put down 3-5%; a typical auto loan costs $50-60K for a car worth ~$15K at payoff.','putting down 3 to 5%',R+'NAR reports a first-time buyer median down payment of ~9% (2024), though 3-5% programs exist. '+C+'$700 x 72 = $50,400, consistent with the lower bound. '+U,[]),
]
